from tnsoc import *
S(10,0,"Raju's grandfather told him about a great war that began with one shot. \"Which war?\" he asked.",
 ["World War I (1914-1918) was fought between the Allied Powers (Britain, France, Russia, later the USA) and the Central Powers (Germany, Austria-Hungary, Turkey). Its long-term causes were militarism, the alliance system, imperialism and nationalism.",
  "The immediate cause was the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria at Sarajevo in 1914. Trench warfare, new weapons and submarines made the war terrible. The war ended with Germany's defeat in 1918.",
  "The Treaty of Versailles (1919) punished Germany with heavy reparations and loss of land. The League of Nations was formed to keep peace, but the harsh terms left bitterness that helped cause the next war."],
 ["militarism","alliance system","Sarajevo","trench warfare","Treaty of Versailles","League of Nations"],
 ("What was the immediate cause of World War I?",["Archduke Franz Ferdinand was killed.","It happened at Sarajevo in 1914.","Austria declared war on Serbia."]),
 ("The League of Nations was formed in 1945.",["It was formed after World War I.","The UN was formed in 1945."]),
 ["WWI: 1914-1918.","Immediate cause: Sarajevo assassination.","Treaty of Versailles 1919.","League of Nations formed."],
 ["m|WWI began in|1914|1939|1905|Sarajevo.","t|Germany was a Central Power.|T|Yes.","m|The treaty that ended WWI was|Versailles|Vienna|Paris 1945|1919.","t|The League of Nations was formed after WWII.|F|After WWI.","m|The assassinated archduke was|Franz Ferdinand|Napoleon|Lenin|At Sarajevo.","s|In which year did WWI end?|1918|November 1918."])
S(10,1,"Meena heard about a time of great hunger and unemployment in the world. \"The Great Depression,\" said her teacher.",
 ["The period between the wars (1919-1939) saw big changes. The Russian Revolution of 1917 under Lenin set up a communist state, and Stalin later led the Soviet Union through planned development.",
  "In 1929 the Great Depression began with the stock market crash in the USA. Banks failed and many lost jobs. President Roosevelt's New Deal tried to revive the economy.",
  "Dictators rose in Europe: Mussolini's fascism in Italy and Hitler's Nazism in Germany. Japan expanded in Asia. These aggressive regimes and the failure of the League of Nations led to war."],
 ["Russian Revolution","Great Depression","New Deal","fascism","Nazism","dictator"],
 ("Why did dictators rise in Europe?",["Economic crisis and unemployment.","Anger over the Versailles Treaty.","People wanted strong leaders."]),
 ("Roosevelt led Germany's Nazi party.",["Hitler led the Nazis.","Roosevelt was the US President."]),
 ["Russian Revolution 1917.","Great Depression began in 1929.","Fascism and Nazism rose.","League of Nations failed."],
 ["m|The Great Depression began in|1929|1914|1945|US crash.","t|Mussolini led Italy.|T|Yes.","m|Roosevelt's plan was the|New Deal|Marshall Plan|Treaty|US recovery.","t|The Depression ended all unemployment.|F|It caused it.","m|Nazism was led by|Hitler|Stalin|Lenin|Germany.","s|Who led the Russian Revolution of 1917?|lenin|Bolsheviks."])
S(10,2,"Asha read about a bomb that changed the world in 1945. \"How did the war begin?\" she asked.",
 ["World War II (1939-1945) began when Germany invaded Poland. It was fought between the Allies (Britain, France, the USSR, the USA) and the Axis (Germany, Italy, Japan). Germany swept through Europe with a fast attack called blitzkrieg.",
  "The war spread when Germany attacked the USSR in 1941 and Japan attacked Pearl Harbor, bringing in the USA. The tide turned at Stalingrad and in the D-Day landings of 1944. Germany surrendered in May 1945.",
  "The USA dropped atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August 1945, and Japan surrendered. Millions were killed, including the Jews in the Holocaust. The cost made the world seek lasting peace."],
 ["Axis and Allies","blitzkrieg","Pearl Harbor","Holocaust","Hiroshima","D-Day"],
 ("How did World War II end?",["Germany surrendered in May 1945.","Atomic bombs fell on Japan.","Japan surrendered in August 1945."]),
 ("Japan was an Allied power.",["Japan was in the Axis.","The Allies fought them."]),
 ["WWII began in 1939.","Axis vs Allies.","Pearl Harbor brought in the USA.","Atom bombs ended the war."],
 ["m|WWII began when Germany invaded|Poland|France|Italy|1939.","t|Japan attacked Pearl Harbor.|T|Yes.","m|Atomic bombs were dropped on|Hiroshima and Nagasaki|Tokyo|Berlin|August 1945.","t|The Axis included Britain.|F|Britain was Allied.","m|The fast German attack was|blitzkrieg|trench war|siege|Lightning war.","s|In which year did WWII end?|1945|Aug-Sep 1945."])
S(10,3,"Raju heard of a long rivalry between two big powers without a direct war. \"The Cold War,\" said the teacher.",
 ["After World War II the United Nations was formed in 1945 to keep peace. Two superpowers, the USA (capitalist) and the USSR (communist), began a rivalry called the Cold War, which did not involve direct fighting between them.",
  "The world split into blocs with NATO led by the USA and the Warsaw Pact led by the USSR. India and other countries followed the Non-Aligned Movement, staying out of both blocs.",
  "Colonies in Asia and Africa gained independence, and the Cold War ended when the USSR broke up in 1991. The Korean and Vietnam wars and the Cuban Missile Crisis were key events."],
 ["United Nations","superpower","Cold War","NATO","Non-Aligned Movement","decolonisation"],
 ("What was the Non-Aligned Movement?",["Countries staying out of both blocs.","India was a founder.","It sought independent foreign policy."]),
 ("The Cold War was a direct war between the USA and USSR.",["No direct war took place.","It was a rivalry."]),
 ["UN formed in 1945.","Cold War: USA vs USSR.","NATO and Warsaw Pact.","USSR broke up in 1991."],
 ["m|The UN was formed in|1945|1919|1991|After WWII.","t|India led the Non-Aligned Movement.|T|Yes.","m|NATO was led by the|USA|USSR|China|Western bloc.","t|The USSR broke up in 1991.|T|Yes.","m|The rivalry without direct war was the|Cold War|World War|Crusade|Superpowers.","s|Which bloc did the USSR lead?|warsaw pact|Eastern bloc."])
S(10,4,"Meena learnt about a reformer who ended a cruel custom in India. \"Raja Ram Mohan Roy,\" said her teacher.",
 ["In the nineteenth century, social and religious reformers worked to end evil customs. Raja Ram Mohan Roy founded the Brahmo Samaj and fought sati, which was banned in 1829. Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar worked for widow remarriage.",
  "Dayananda Saraswati started the Arya Samaj and Ramakrishna and Vivekananda spread the message of service. Sir Syed Ahmad Khan promoted modern education among Muslims at Aligarh. Jyotiba Phule fought caste discrimination.",
  "In Tamil Nadu, Vallalar Ramalinga Adigal taught compassion, Iyothee Thass worked for the oppressed, and Muthulakshmi Reddy and others worked for women. The reforms led to laws and a new social awakening."],
 ["Brahmo Samaj","sati","Arya Samaj","Vidyasagar","Phule","Vallalar"],
 ("What did Raja Ram Mohan Roy achieve?",["He founded the Brahmo Samaj.","He fought against sati.","Sati was banned in 1829."]),
 ("Vivekananda founded the Brahmo Samaj.",["Raja Ram Mohan Roy did.","Vivekananda spread Vedanta."]),
 ["Reformers fought evil customs.","Sati banned in 1829.","Vidyasagar: widow remarriage.","Phule: against caste bias."],
 ["m|Sati was banned in|1829|1857|1947|Reform.","t|Vidyasagar supported widow remarriage.|T|Yes.","m|The Arya Samaj was founded by|Dayananda Saraswati|Roy|Phule|Reform movement.","t|Phule worked against caste bias.|T|Yes.","m|The Brahmo Samaj was founded by|Raja Ram Mohan Roy|Tilak|Gandhi|Reform.","s|Which Tamil saint taught compassion?|vallalar|Ramalinga Adigal."])
S(10,5,"Raju saw a statue of a brave Tamil fighter. \"Did the south revolt against the British?\" he asked.",
 ["Before 1857, Tamil Nadu saw early revolts against the British East India Company. Pulithevar of Nerkattumseval fought against the Company's tax claims. Veerapandiya Kattabomman of Panchalankurichi refused to pay tax and was hanged in 1799.",
  "Marudhu Brothers of Sivaganga led a revolt and issued the Tiruchirappalli Proclamation calling for unity against the British. Dheeran Chinnamalai of Kongu country fought with the support of Tipu Sultan's methods.",
  "The Vellore Revolt of 1806 was a rising of Indian soldiers against changes in dress that hurt their religious feelings. Though crushed, these revolts showed early resistance."],
 ["Pulithevar","Kattabomman","Marudhu Brothers","Vellore Revolt","East India Company","Poligar"],
 ("What caused the Vellore Revolt of 1806?",["New dress rules hurt religious feelings.","Soldiers rose against the British.","It was an early revolt."]),
 ("Kattabomman paid his tax gladly.",["He refused to pay tax.","He was hanged in 1799."]),
 ["Pulithevar: early resistance.","Kattabomman refused tax.","Marudhu Brothers: proclamation.","Vellore Revolt 1806."],
 ["m|The Vellore Revolt was in|1806|1857|1919|Soldiers.","t|Kattabomman resisted the Company.|T|Yes.","m|Panchalankurichi was ruled by|Kattabomman|Marudhu|Tipu|Poligar.","t|The Marudhu Brothers were from Sivaganga.|T|Yes.","m|Dheeran Chinnamalai fought in|Kongu country|Madurai|Chennai|West Tamil Nadu.","s|In which year was Kattabomman hanged?|1799|Revolt."])
S(10,6,"Meena read about the revolt of 1857. \"Was that the first fight for freedom?\" she asked.",
 ["The Revolt of 1857 began with sepoys at Meerut and spread across north and central India. Causes included greased cartridges, annexation by the Doctrine of Lapse, high taxes and loss of power for Indian rulers. Leaders were Rani Lakshmibai, Nana Sahib, Tantia Tope and Bahadur Shah Zafar.",
  "The revolt failed due to lack of unity and British strength. After it, the rule of the Company ended and the British Crown took over in 1858.",
  "Later the Indian National Congress was founded in 1885 by A. O. Hume and others. The early leaders, called Moderates, used petitions, and the Extremists, like Tilak, wanted bolder action. The Swadeshi movement followed the Partition of Bengal in 1905."],
 ["Revolt of 1857","Doctrine of Lapse","Rani Lakshmibai","Indian National Congress","Moderates and Extremists","Swadeshi"],
 ("What changed after the Revolt of 1857?",["The Company's rule ended.","The Crown took over in 1858.","Policy toward Indian rulers changed."]),
 ("The Indian National Congress was founded in 1857.",["It was founded in 1885.","1857 is the Revolt."]),
 ["1857 revolt began at Meerut.","Crown rule from 1858.","Congress founded in 1885.","Swadeshi after the Partition of Bengal."],
 ["m|The Congress was founded in|1885|1857|1905|By Hume.","t|Tilak was an Extremist leader.|T|Yes.","m|The Partition of Bengal was in|1905|1885|1919|Led to Swadeshi.","t|The Revolt of 1857 began at Meerut.|T|Yes.","m|After 1858 India was ruled by the|British Crown|Company|Mughals|Direct rule.","s|Which queen fought in 1857?|lakshmibai|Jhansi."])
S(10,7,"Asha read about a man in a simple dhoti who led millions with truth. \"Gandhi,\" said the teacher.",
 ["Mahatma Gandhi returned from South Africa in 1915 and led the freedom movement on the principles of truth and non-violence (satyagraha). He led the Champaran and Kheda satyagrahas and the Ahmedabad mill strike.",
  "He launched the Non-Cooperation Movement in 1920 after the Jallianwala Bagh massacre of 1919 and the Rowlatt Act. It was called off after Chauri Chaura. The Civil Disobedience Movement began in 1930 with the Salt March to Dandi.",
  "The Quit India Movement of 1942 called for 'Do or Die'. Subhas Chandra Bose formed the Indian National Army. India became independent on 15 August 1947."],
 ["satyagraha","Non-Cooperation","Jallianwala Bagh","Salt March","Quit India","INA"],
 ("What was the Salt March?",["Gandhi marched to Dandi in 1930.","He broke the salt law.","It began Civil Disobedience."]),
 ("The Quit India Movement was in 1920.",["It was in 1942.","1920 was Non-Cooperation."]),
 ["Satyagraha: truth and non-violence.","Non-Cooperation 1920.","Salt March 1930.","Quit India 1942; freedom 1947."],
 ["m|The Salt March went to|Dandi|Delhi|Madras|1930.","t|Quit India said 'Do or Die'.|T|Yes.","m|Non-Cooperation began in|1920|1942|1930|After Jallianwala Bagh.","t|India became free on 15 August 1947.|T|Yes.","m|The INA was formed by|Subhas Chandra Bose|Nehru|Tilak|Azad Hind.","s|Which massacre took place in 1919?|jallianwala bagh|Amritsar."])
S(10,8,"Raju heard Tamil patriots sung about in a song. \"Who fought for freedom in Tamil Nadu?\" he asked.",
 ["Tamil Nadu played a big part in the freedom struggle. V. O. Chidambaram Pillai started the Swadeshi Steam Navigation Company at Thoothukudi to challenge British shipping. Subramania Bharati inspired people with patriotic songs.",
  "Subramania Siva, Vanchinathan, who killed the collector Ashe, and Vanchi Aiyar were revolutionaries. S. Satyamurti and Rajaji led the Congress, and the Vedaranyam Salt March of 1930 led by Rajaji was held in the Cauvery delta.",
  "Women like Dr Muthulakshmi Reddy, Rukmini Lakshmipathi, Ambujammal and Vedaratnam took part, and Tiruppur Kumaran gave his life with the national flag. Kamaraj, a leader of the Congress, later became a great Chief Minister."],
 ["V. O. Chidambaram Pillai","Bharati","Vedaranyam Salt March","Rajaji","Tiruppur Kumaran","Kamaraj"],
 ("How did V. O. Chidambaram Pillai challenge the British?",["He began a Swadeshi shipping company.","It competed with British ships.","He was jailed for it."]),
 ("The Vedaranyam march was led by Kamaraj.",["It was led by Rajaji.","Kamaraj was a later leader."]),
 ["V.O.C. started Swadeshi shipping.","Bharati's songs inspired.","Vedaranyam march 1930.","Women also took part."],
 ["m|The Swadeshi shipping company was started by|V. O. Chidambaram Pillai|Bharati|Rajaji|At Thoothukudi.","t|Bharati wrote patriotic songs.|T|Yes.","m|The Vedaranyam March was led by|Rajaji|Gandhi|Nehru|1930.","t|Tiruppur Kumaran died holding the flag.|T|Yes.","m|Vanchinathan was a|revolutionary|king|poet only|Killed Ashe.","s|Where was the Vedaranyam march held?|vedaranyam|Near Nagapattinam."])
S(10,9,"Meena saw a poster of a leader fighting for equal rights. \"Periyar,\" said her teacher.",
 ["Tamil Nadu saw great social change in the twentieth century. The Justice Party, formed in 1916, worked for greater representation for non-Brahmins in jobs and education. Periyar E. V. Ramasamy led the Self-Respect Movement from 1925 against caste and superstition.",
  "Women's rights were pushed by Muthulakshmi Reddy, who worked to end the devadasi system, and by the Self-Respect Movement. Reforms included temple entry, free education and the mid-day meal scheme.",
  "Leaders such as Dr Ambedkar's thoughts, Iyothee Thass and later Kamaraj, Annadurai and Karunanidhi shaped Tamil Nadu's welfare policies, including reservation and scholarships."],
 ["Justice Party","Self-Respect Movement","Periyar","devadasi system","temple entry","social justice"],
 ("What did the Self-Respect Movement aim for?",["Equality and dignity for all.","An end to caste and superstition.","Rights for women."]),
 ("Periyar started the Justice Party.",["He led the Self-Respect Movement.","The Justice Party began in 1916."]),
 ["Justice Party 1916.","Self-Respect Movement 1925.","Women's rights and reforms.","Welfare policies for all."],
 ["m|The Self-Respect Movement was led by|Periyar|Kamaraj|Rajaji|From 1925.","t|Muthulakshmi Reddy worked against devadasi system.|T|Yes.","m|The Justice Party began in|1916|1947|1925|Early 20th century.","t|The Self-Respect Movement supported caste.|F|It opposed caste.","m|The mid-day meal scheme helps|school attendance|taxes|roads|Welfare.","s|Which leader is called Thanthai Periyar?|e. v. ramasamy|Periyar."])
